@@ -0,0 +1,217 @@
+# SHRDLU on the SIMH PDP-1: experimental compatibility prototype
+
+Eve Quinn / LittleMouseQuinn, 2026-11-02; updated 2026-11-05.
+
+This is an **experimental** compatibility path for Bill Ezell's PDP-1
+SHRDLU. It changes the guest-side DCS2 calls to use SIMH's original Type
+630 character IOTs, preserving the original DCS2 build behind the
+`SIMH_COMPAT` switch.
+
+**Important:** An unmodified current SIMH PDP-1 does **not** provide
+the required Type 340 host adapter. The 2026-11-02 tests used Eve's separate
+local experimental SIMH branch `eve/type340-dac-width`, commit `f963dea`,
+which supplies Type 340 display IOTs, single-channel PDP-1D sequence breaks,
+full-width display DMA, and a partial BBN clock countdown extension.
+This is not yet a fully upstreamable stock-SIMH SHRDLU port.
+
+## Reproduce the verified text/dictionary/overlay test
+
+The November 5 regression was run against `wjenh/pidp1-mods` commit
+`b4f6f40` (November 4 Sketchpad, SHRDLU color labels and Life release),
+with this shim applied as a separate local source commit. The earlier
+November 2 test used `caa0c76` (the initial SHRDLU release).
+Build `am1` and `ovlload` from the checked-out repository first.
+
+From `FunStuff/Shrdlu/Shrdlu`, run:
+
+```sh
+make simh AM1=/path/to/am1 OVLLOAD=/path/to/ovlload
+```
+
+The target assembles `shrdlu-simh.rim`, writes its matching
+`shrdlu-simh.ovl` and `shrdlu-simh-drum.img`, and runs `ovlload -c`
+to verify that all 13 overlays match. The original `make` target
+can still produce native DCS2 `shrdlu.rim` from the same source.
+
+From repository root:
+
+```sh
+python3 tests/run_shrdlu_simh_smoke.py --simh /path/to/experimental-SIMH/BIN/pdp1
+```
+
+The script starts an isolated PDP-1D48/64K simulation, enables single-channel
+SBS and the experimental Type 340 and clock, attaches the overlay drum and
+Type 630 DCS, and uses a real TCP session. It checks:
+
+1. the full SHRDLU server banner;
+2. `/WORD BLOCK` => `BLOCK: NOUN NS`, with **single**, not duplicate, echo;
+3. `/ROOM` => arena statistics (loads the commands overlay);
+4. `ARE THERE ANY BLOCKS?` => the correct five-block answer, with a
+   completed response and prompt.
+
+The reported answer exactly matched the standalone Modern C reference on
+2026-11-02, including enumeration and punctuation, after normalizing Telnet
+line endings. This is a limited one-question comparison, not a general
+equivalence proof.
+
+### Device compatibility details
+
+- In stock Type 630, `RCC` reads a character **and unlocks the scanner**.
+  DCS2 `RCH` does not have the same semantics. The SIMH shim substitutes
+  the former at the two reader call sites.
+- The first character received is a session-start indication because stock
+  SIMH does not expose DCS2's TCP-connection-status bit to the guest.
+- Stock Type 630 echoes received bytes itself, so the DCS2 guest-side echo
+  is omitted under `SIMH_COMPAT`.
+- The Type 630 scanner status is shared by input-ready and transmit-complete.
+  A single shared transmit helper polls the completion and acknowledges it
+  before another character is sent. It is called across the PDP-1 banks through
+  a full-address `JSP I`. Current callers do not depend on preserving AC.
+- This is presently a single-connection, one-line smoke path. DCS2's socket
+  negotiation, close/rebind behavior, Telnet-option fidelity and large-volume
+  traffic are not proven equivalent.
+
+### Graphics and remaining failures
+
+The genuine PDP-1 program rendered a 1024x1024 Type 340 blocks-world image
+through a separately instrumented *headless* SIMH backend. In the November 2
+SIMH_COMPAT build, the captured PGM held **10,035 lit pixels** and the display
+reported 121,396 stop/edge interrupts in a bounded three-second run.
+This verifies real display-list execution, **not** a live SDL graphics
+window, mouse, phosphor aging, or interactive light pen.
+
+The motion command `PICK UP A BIG RED BLOCK.` was correctly read and echoed
+as words, but **did not produce the final answer** during a 28-second bounded
+test. The interrupted PC was at `005170`, the `handShow` loop waiting for
+`hpPend` to be cleared by display-frame/hand application processing.
+Timer and display scheduling remain under investigation.
+
+Other unverified areas include the full 42-sentence demonstration, all
+overlay load/eviction paths, actual hand motion, restart after connection
+loss, and save/state fidelity. Do not call this a complete port yet.
+
+
+### Bounded hand-motion diagnostic
+
+The original PDP-1 program supports `FASTMOTION`: it displays the final
+hand position immediately instead of performing each intermediate paced
+step. That is useful for separating language, planning and display-hand
+updates from the still-unverified timing of normal animation.
+
+From `FunStuff/Shrdlu/Shrdlu`, build the **separate** variant:
+
+```sh
+make simh-fastmotion AM1=/path/to/am1 OVLLOAD=/path/to/ovlload
+```
+
+From the repository root:
+
+```sh
+python3 tests/run_shrdlu_simh_smoke.py --simh /path/to/experimental-SIMH/BIN/pdp1
+  --prefix shrdlu-simh-fast --motion --timeout 35
+```
+
+The motion assertion requires the PDP-1 guest to parse
+`PICK UP A BIG RED BLOCK.` and return `OK.` with its prompt. It does not
+prove the graphic intermediate frames or light-pen interaction, and needs
+the same *experimental* Type 340 adapter as the ordinary smoke test.
+The standard `simh` build and its uncompromised paced path remain separate.
+
+
+On November 5, this fast-motion variant passed all five test cases twice.
+The normal paced-motion build still passed the original four text/overlay
+cases, but its hand movement was not completed in a bounded probe.
+A guest-state diagnostic, `tests/diag_shrdlu_motion.py`, can capture the
+PDP-1 state after attempting the paced move:
+
+```sh
+python3 tests/diag_shrdlu_motion.py --simh /path/to/experimental-SIMH/BIN/pdp1 \
+  --input-timeout 45 --post-words 30
+```
+
+In the November 5 normal-motion probe the command parsed but stopped
+inside `handShow`, with `hpPend=1` and `hpDue` one guest millisecond ahead
+of `clT`. Display frame count was advancing. This is evidence of a possible
+pacing or clock throughput issue, **not proof of a permanent deadlock**.
+The complete normal animation and real SDL display remain open.
+
+
+### November 8: Type 630 completion race and a bounded paced-motion probe
+
+Bill clarified in an email that the original t30dpy mouse updates are sent
+asynchronously at approximately 8 ms intervals, independently of guest polling.
+The blincolnlights `src/blincolnlights/pdp1/display.c` worker uses a
+nonblocking display socket, retains the last pen position until lifted,
+and applies motion filters. These observations concern the original display
+pipeline; this headless SIMH test does **not** reproduce its asynchronous
+mouse socket or certify prediction fidelity.
+
+A separate issue emerged on the guest terminal path. With a hand-movement
+test using larger screen-space steps, the PDP-1 sometimes returned `K.`
+instead of `OK.` even though it reached its prompt. Instrumenting both
+SIMH Type 630's TCB IOT entry and its delayed line service showed:
+
+- After the long motion, the guest issued a TCB for `O`, while an old
+  scanner-ready flag was present. The following TCB for `K` occurred before
+  any completion service for `O`, overwriting the same one-line transmit
+  buffer.
+- This guest shim now uses Type 630 **TCC** to send and release the scanner
+  lock together, then waits for a **new** completion before sending the next
+  byte. The distinction matters because Type 630 shares its receiver and
+  transmitter ready flag. This implementation is intentionally limited to a
+  **single DCS line**; multi-line output must be designed and tested separately.
+
+To reproduce the bounded path that revealed the bug without modifying
+the ordinary hand animation, use the optional **coarse paced** build:
+
+```sh
+cd FunStuff/Shrdlu/Shrdlu
+make simh-paced-probe AM1=/path/to/am1 OVLLOAD=/path/to/ovlload
+cd ../../..
+python3 tests/run_shrdlu_simh_smoke.py \
+  --simh /path/to/experimental-SIMH/BIN/pdp1 \
+  --prefix shrdlu-simh-paced --motion --timeout 80
+```
+
+`simh-paced-probe` exercises the original paced `leg`/hand posting path
+with 512-pixel steps. It is **not** `FASTMOTION`, but it is also not a
+fidelity test of smooth hand movement. The normal build retains its original
+step sizes. The motion test insists on the exact complete `OK.` and prompt:
+a truncated `K.` reply is a failure, even if the position was applied.
+
+The Type 630 IOT traces were produced in a separate local, **unpublished**
+debug-instrumented host worktree; no temporary host tracing code is required
+by this guest change. This result should be independently reproduced against
+the uninstrumented experimental SIMH binary before considering integration.
+
+### November 8 current-upstream reproducibility checkpoint
+
+The source patch and Type 630 first-byte correction above were cherry-picked
+without conflicts onto Bill's updated `pidp1-mods` base
+`ec2cee7cc87dd9ab5b012d4b05cf248df66a30c2` (November 8, 2026).
+The eight changed paths are guest-side source, Makefile, tests, and this
+README only. No generated RIM, OVL, drum, or instrumented host binaries are
+included in the patch.
+
+For this checkpoint the independent experimental PDP-1 Type 340 SIMH host
+source, `f963dea32000956d87a5b910c6ce3760ad325c74`, was freshly rebuilt
+from a clean tracked tree on November 8 and passed SIMH's internal register
+sanity check. With that resulting host binary, the new guest source produced:
+
+- normal stock-Type630-compat guest: **4/4** live DCS TCP assertions passed;
+- opt-in FASTMOTION guest: **5/5**, including complete pick-up reply;
+- opt-in coarse paced guest: **5/5**, including complete `OK.\n*\n`;
+- all **13** drum overlays checked as current in **each** SIMH build;
+- original non-`SIMH_COMPAT` DCS2 guest assembled successfully.
+
+These smoke tests use an existing earlier AM1 executable. The current
+upstream AM1 assembler source was not rebuilt here because the test host
+lacks `bison` and `flex`; no claim is made about its new compiler changes.
+
+Bill's current `Docs/UsingType340Display.md` says `DRP` is deprecated and
+identical to `DRC`, with motion prediction performed in the emulator's
+central display logic. Our experimental SIMH host does **not** yet implement
+that prediction pipeline. An asynchronous real mouse stream, default-step
+paced motion, and human tracking at high speed are **not** validated by these
+headless tests. None of these results is an upstream merge or a complete
+visual/interactive port.
